# 5. Important Theoretical Questions with Answers

These questions cover the fundamental concepts of your assignment.

### Basic Questions

**Q1. What is the N-Queens Problem?**

It is a problem of placing N Queens on an N × N chessboard such that no two Queens attack each other.

**Q2. What is Backtracking?**

Backtracking is an algorithmic technique that tries possible solutions and goes back whenever a choice cannot lead to a valid solution.

**Q3. Why is N-Queens called a Backtracking problem?**

Because whenever a Queen cannot be placed safely, the algorithm removes the previously placed Queen and tries another position.

**Q4. What is a chessboard matrix?**

It is a two-dimensional array of size N × N used to represent the chessboard.

**Q5. What does N represent?**

N represents the number of Queens and the number of rows and columns in the chessboard.

**Q6. How many Queens are placed in an N × N chessboard?**

Exactly N Queens.

**Q7. What are the three attacking directions of a Queen?**

Horizontal, vertical and diagonal.

**Q8. What is a safe position?**

A position where a Queen cannot attack any previously placed Queen.

**Q9. What is an unsafe position?**

A position where a Queen can attack another already placed Queen.

**Q10. What happens when no safe position is available?**

The algorithm backtracks to the previous row and tries another column.

### Backtracking-Related Questions

**Q11. What are the basic steps of Backtracking?**

Select a choice, check whether it is valid, proceed if valid, otherwise undo the choice and try another option.

**Q12. What is the purpose of Backtracking?**

To systematically explore possible solutions while avoiding choices that cannot lead to a valid solution.

**Q13. Is Backtracking a recursive technique?**

Yes. It is commonly implemented using recursion because the algorithm repeatedly solves smaller versions of the problem.

**Q14. What is the base condition in N-Queens?**

When all N Queens have been successfully placed, the algorithm returns success.

**Q15. What happens if the algorithm cannot place all Queens?**

It returns failure after exploring all possible arrangements.

**Q16. Why do we place one Queen per row?**

Because no two Queens can occupy the same row.

**Q17. Can two Queens be placed in the same column?**

No, because they would attack each other vertically.

**Q18. What is the difference between Recursion and Backtracking?**

Recursion is a technique in which a function calls itself. Backtracking is a problem-solving technique that uses trial, validation and undoing of choices.

**Q19. Can Backtracking find all possible solutions?**

Yes. By continuing the search after finding a solution, it can generate all valid solutions.

**Q20. Is the N-Queens problem always solvable?**

For standard N-Queens, solutions exist for N = 1 and all N ≥ 4. There are no solutions for N = 2 and N = 3.

